{:schema :kotoba.text-migration/v1
 :profile :kotoba.regex/bounded-v1
 :date "2026-09-08"
 :legacy
 {:source "src/kotoba/lang/text.cljk"
  :status :retained-general-oracle
  :note "kotoba.lang.text keeps the HOST-regex wrappers (re-find / re-matches
         / re-seq / replace / replace-first). They stay: they are the general
         oracle, they accept the whole host dialect, and a `.cljc` caller on
         the JVM has no reason to give them up. What changes is that a
         `.kotoba` GUEST is no longer without any regex at all."}
 :canonical
 {:source "src/kotoba/lang/bounded_regex.kotoba"
  :authority :kotoba
  :required-capabilities []}

 :why-this-was-allowed
 {:classification :not-yet-implemented
  :authority "90-docs/adr/2608650000 (root): regex appears in neither
              :forbidden-heads nor lang/surface-status.edn. The single
              occurrence of :regex, in lang/value-codec.edn :rejected-closed,
              says a COMPILED PATTERN cannot be transferred as a VALUE -- a
              canonical-encoding decision, not a ban on the operation."
  :respected "A pattern here is a :string argument. Nothing acquires a regex
              type, so nothing new has to become encodable."
  :reader "lang/compat.edn :measured :regex-literal -- a #\"...\" literal in
           guest source is a reader rejection, so no source in this subset can
           be reached by writing one."}

 :bound
 {:hazard "backtracking, not notation. A backtracking engine turns (a+)+$ into
           exponential work on caller-chosen input."
  :met-by "the subset is group-free, so a branch is a linear chain of pieces
           and its Glushkov automaton is a chain of states. The state set is a
           bitmask in one i64 and one code point advances all of it in one
           forward walk. There is no backtracking stack to add."
  :pieces-per-branch 60
  :shielding-axis :resource-bounds}

 :subset
 {:supported [:literal :dot :class :class-range :class-negation
              :escape-d :escape-D :escape-w :escape-W :escape-s :escape-S
              :escape-n :escape-t :escape-r :escape-literal
              :star :plus :question :anchor-start :anchor-end :alternation]
  :refused [:group :backreference :counted-repetition :lookaround
            :non-greedy :named-class :unicode-property]
  :refusal-is "the error arm of [:result :i64 :string], never a 0 -- so
               `outside the subset` cannot be read as `no match`."}

 :exports
 [:regex-piece-budget :regex-valid? :regex-contains :regex-match
  :regex-find-index :regex-match-end :regex-count]

 :divergences
 {:bracket-first-rbracket "`[]]` matches `]` -- POSIX, grep and Python agree
                           (measured: printf 'a]b' | grep -c '[]]' -> 1);
                           JavaScript RegExp does not. Asserted, not skipped."
  :leftmost-longest "alternation is leftmost-longest, not leftmost-first.
                     `a|ab` on \"ab\" matches \"ab\" here, \"a\" in a
                     backtracking engine."
  :ascii-classes "\\d is [0-9], \\w is [0-9A-Za-z_], \\s is the six ASCII
                  spaces bounded_text's ws? names. Named rather than silently
                  differing from a host engine on Unicode digits."
  :dot-excludes-newline "as in grep."}

 :evidence
 {:compiled #{:web :wasm32}
  :amu "9092ee34 (origin/main, 2026-09-08)"
  :self-check {:form "failures * 1000 + checks-run"
               :measured 47
               :meaning "0 failures over 47 checks. A build that ran nothing
                         answers 0, which is visibly different."}
  :independent-oracle {:harness "scripts/verify-bounded-regex.cljk"
                       :oracle "the host's own RegExp -- not a mirror
                                implementation, so a disagreement is real"
                       :comparisons 4640
                       :mismatches "3, all of them the []] divergence above"}
  :discrimination {:broke "atom-matches? -- made `.` also match newline"
                   :self-check-went "47 -> 1047 (one failure, 47 still ran)"
                   :parity-went "3 -> 6 mismatches, the three new ones exactly
                                 the newline cases"
                   :note "both checks were seen to fail for the reason they
                          name, not merely to be red."}}

 :cost
 {:algorithm "O(n * m), m <= 60"
  :find-index "O(n^2 * m): a leftmost START needs a per-start answer and this
               kernel has nowhere to keep one start offset per live state.
               regex-contains is the O(n * m) answer to the question grep asks."
  :backend-residue
  "The web backend delivers quadratic n regardless of the algorithm:
   string-code-point-at at a byte offset appears to cost O(offset) there.
   Measured 2026-09-08, regex-contains with a ONE-piece pattern:
   7.5 / 14 / 41 / 144 / 550 ms at n = 500 / 1000 / 2000 / 4000 / 8000, and a
   five-piece pattern costs nearly the same -- the growth is in the text
   access, not the pattern walk. CONTROL placing it outside this module:
   bounded_text's index-of-text, which predates this file, draws the same
   curve (7.3 / 23 / 60 / 249 / 881 ms). Same defect class amu fixed in the
   NATIVE loader on 2026-08-31 (checked_string_code_point_at revalidating the
   whole string per access; strings domain 10.77x -> 2.17x vs its gcc twin).
   The web backend still has the shape. Not fixable from here."}

 :abi-facts-learned
 {:max-parameters "5, and it counts a loop's captured outer variables too
                   (:kotoba.error/max-parameters,
                   :kotoba.error/loop-parameter-ceiling). This is why a branch
                   is carried as its own SUBSTRING rather than as
                   (pattern, start, end). Authority:
                   kotoba-lang docs/adr/ADR-reliability-t54-max-parameters.md
                   -- keep 5, use records; raising it needs a new security ADR
                   with dual-backend evidence and threat review."
  :bit-ops "bit-and / bit-or compile on wasm32 and are rejected on web
            (unsupported KIR operation), so the bitmask is arithmetic."
  :shift "i64-shift-left requires a LITERAL count in [0,63]."
  :quot-rem "quot admitted, rem not."
  :fuel "the budget is declared in the policy file as {:budgets {:fuel n}};
         the emitted web artifact bakes 512 when none is given."
  :utf8-trap "string-code-point-at traps off a code-point boundary. The '$'
              anchor is found by WALKING pieces, not by indexing e-1, because
              e-1 is a continuation byte whenever the branch ends in a
              multi-byte character (measured: `é+` trapped there)."}

 :not-done
 [{:what "regex-driven split / replace"
   :why "both need a collection return or a builder loop; replace can be
         written on top of regex-match-end and string-concat, and is the
         obvious next tranche. Not started, not blocked."}
  {:what "capture groups"
   :why "groups are refused by this subset on purpose -- they are what makes a
         chain into a tree, and the linear-time guarantee is a property of the
         chain. A capturing engine is a different design, not a widening."}
  {:what "a CLJC mirror of this kernel"
   :why "deliberately absent. The parity oracle is the HOST's RegExp, which is
         independent; a mirror written by the same hand would agree with the
         kernel's mistakes."}]}
